{
  "dom_id": "step-e270",
  "name": "Example: Interpreting the Derivative of a Function",
  "problem": "Consider a spherical balloon with radius $r.$ As the balloon loses air, its volume $V$ decreases. What is the best interpretation of $V'(r)?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930198/assets/7f52288ca4670375413ecd5283bc2d15fbb332af311ead07986c1526d25e338a.png)",
  "worked_solution": "The function $V(r)$ gives the volume of a sphere with radius $r.$ So\n\n$$\nV'(r) = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}V}{\\textrm{d}r} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Change in }V}{\\textrm{Change in }r}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $V'(r)$ is the rate at which the volume changes as the radius changes.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
